 solicitors  winner was Taylor Carter for golf and  runner-up was Christian Brown athletics  and castle point sponsored by a pinnacle  PSG club of the year winner hadley  mountain bike club two runners-up castle  point joggers and porters sock ability  football club school of the year  sponsored by affinity 1777 winner was  the danes school two runners-up kensal  infants academy and thundersley primary  school services to sport respect award  sponsored by the co-operative  funeralcare winner nigel Stewart  football and runner-up Naomi palea  swimming castlepoint Borough Council  mayor's award winner Abby  language disability sports personality  of the year sponsored by waters and  Stanton limited winner James right for  swimming runner-up Matthew Hamilton for  athletics young sports personality of  the year sponsored by southend united  community and educational trust winner  laura been archery and two runners-up  alex body for archery and hayden skinner  for gymnastics sports personality of the  year sponsored by worlds legal  solicitors winner was to lead ethan  football basketball and athletics lauren  ban archery stay well this winter now's  the time to get your free flu jab this  week all those it at risk groups are  being reminded to get their free flu jab  adults over 65 those with long-term  health conditions children aged two to  seven carers and pregnant women may all  be eligible the reminder is being issued  by NHS England public health England the  Department of Health the NHS trust  development agency and monitor every  year hundreds of thousands of people see  their GPS and tens of thousands are  hospitalized because of flu symptoms of  flu can be very unpleasant and last for  several weeks it can lead to more  serious complications like middle ear  infections in children and pneumonia or  bronchitis for those with underlying  health conditions and in some cases it  can be fatal Trisha Dorsey chief nurse  at NHS castle point and Rochford  clinical commissioning group CCG said  flu is a highly contagious illness that  spreads rapidly through population it  can cause serious complications for  at-risk groups such as the elderly  expectant mothers and people with  long-term health conditions this year  more children are being vaccinated  because we know that they can be very  easily picked up germs and spread them  amongst friends and family members this  vaccination program helps protect the  population tritiated my advice for  anyone eligible for the free flu  vaccination is to get  now before flu starts to circulate in  the community although it is not  possible to know which strains of flu  will circulate each winter the flu  vaccination is still the best protection  against an unpredictable virus which can  cause severe illness and deaths amongst  vulnerable groups throughout the last  decade there has generally been a good  match between the strains of flu in the  vaccine and those that subsequently  circulate so it's important that we do  all we can to ensure venerable people  are not discouraged from having flu  vaccinations now or in the future  vaccines are available between October  2015 and March 2016 says contact your  local GP or pharmacy now it's free  because you need it for more information  visit www.fema.gov the year in the  National primary care awards 2015 dr.  Roger Luke Gardner ccg governing body  member and local GP chief nurse Trisha  Dawson Debbie Vic head of financial  management and planning attended the  complimentary awards ceremony that took  place in London on 8th of October the  award was presented by Julie would chief  executive of NHS clinical commissioners  criteria for the award included evidence  of great leadership with both clinical  and managerial leadership seen  throughout the organization and  successfully navigation through  performance quality improvement and  financial pressures the CCG was  recognized for having a golden thread  through its strategic and operational  plans to demonstrate how it is focusing  on local patient and population needs  and service transformation to improve  local health outcomes the CCG was  nominated for its pioneering preventive  care coordinations service that delivers  care to  more vulnerable patients in Castle Point  and Rochford who may be at risk of  decline the award celebrated CCGs who  have gone the extra mile with respect to  integrated working moving away from the  archaic model of working individually  working in partnership with GP practices  the new service will identify those  patients whose health they deemed to be  at risk of decline predominantly the  frail elderly and those with long term  conditions instead ssin accountable  officer for NHS castlepoint and rockford  ccg said this is truly fantastic news we  have something special here at the  castle point and Rochford ccg a great  mix of clinical leaders and managers who  are truly passionate about improving the  health and well-being of our local  community we are also lucky to have  great relationships with all of our  partners including those delivering  local services the voluntary sector and  local authorities we look forward to  building on this with mutual aims to  make things better for local residents  Rochford district matters is designed  and published by Rochford District  Council the telephone number for the  council is 0 1 702 546 366 and this  recording has been produced by  volunteers from the rayleigh Rochford  and castle point talking newspaper
 

